 ahead of Guckian by 22 to 34 points", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win at 95 to 98 percent", "Independent Ken Block runs second, making the contest closer than the partisan margin suggests"], "rationale": "An open seat in a state leaning Democratic by eight points, a fractured opposition, and unanimous expert and market agreement point clearly to Foulkes, whom every recent poll leads by more than twenty points over Guckian. Block's independent bid narrows her path to a majority but not to victory.", "watch": "Ken Block consolidating anti-Foulkes voters could compress the race to single digits and put the independent in genuine contention.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Dan McKee (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governorship is open this year after Dan McKee lost the Democratic nomination to Helena Foulkes. Foulkes faces Republican Aaron Guckian and independent Ken Block in a state that has leaned Democratic by about eight points. Every poll since August shows her ahead of Guckian by 22 to 34 points, and a late-October survey put her up eleven on Block, the strongest of the independents.\n\nAll eight major race ratings place the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets put Democratic odds at 95 percent or better. Guckian has drawn an elections complaint over a private flight, while Foulkes has faced questions about a late policy announcement. The main residual uncertainty is whether Block can consolidate opposition voters; as it stands, the Democratic nominee is strongly favored to hold the office for the party.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 18.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.997, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9964, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9953, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9924, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_de